WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.707
To insert a key v, first search for it.

00:00:03.707 --> 00:00:07.114
Say we insert 13 into this tree.

00:00:07.114 --> 00:00:09.997
The search goes right at 12

00:00:09.997 --> 00:00:13.055
left at 18, left at 15

00:00:13.055 --> 00:00:15.151
and falls off the tree

00:00:15.151 --> 00:00:17.510
15 has no left child.

00:00:17.598 --> 00:00:20.480
That empty spot is the only place

00:00:20.568 --> 00:00:23.888
where 13 can go without breaking

00:00:23.975 --> 00:00:25.460
the BST property.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.282
So the new node goes there

00:00:28.282 --> 00:00:29.930
and it is a leaf.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:34.051
Nothing else in the tree moves: no shifting

00:00:34.051 --> 00:00:37.530
as in an array, and no restructuring.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.670
There is one catch.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.543
The search pointer x ends up null

00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:44.513
and null cannot tell us

00:00:44.595 --> 00:00:46.400
whose child to change.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.273
So we walk down with two pointers.

00:00:49.273 --> 00:00:51.161
x is the current node.

00:00:51.161 --> 00:00:53.213
y, the trailing pointer

00:00:53.213 --> 00:00:55.430
is always the parent of x.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.056
13 is larger than 12

00:00:58.056 --> 00:01:01.750
y moves to 12, x goes right to 18.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.316
13 is smaller than 18

00:01:05.316 --> 00:01:09.573
y moves to 18, x goes left to 15.

00:01:09.573 --> 00:01:12.789
13 is smaller than 15

00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:16.289
y moves to 15, and x goes left

00:01:16.289 --> 00:01:17.140
to null.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.222
Now x is null, and y is 15

00:01:20.222 --> 00:01:23.551
y is the node that gets the new child.

00:01:23.551 --> 00:01:26.454
13 is smaller than 15

00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:28.844
so the new node becomes the

00:01:28.929 --> 00:01:30.807
left child of 15.

00:01:30.807 --> 00:01:34.222
If the tree was empty, y is still null

00:01:34.222 --> 00:01:37.210
and the new node becomes the root.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.962
In Java, insert first makes the new node z.

00:01:41.962 --> 00:01:46.230
y starts as null, and x starts at the root.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.854
While x is not null, y catches up with x

00:01:50.958 --> 00:01:53.982
and x moves left if z.key

00:01:54.086 --> 00:01:56.796
is smaller than x.key

00:01:56.796 --> 00:01:58.986
and right otherwise.

00:01:58.986 --> 00:02:02.217
When the loop ends, x is null

00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:06.700
and y is the last node on the search path.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.759
Then z.parent is set to y.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.569
If y is null, the tree was empty

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:14.552
and z becomes the root.

00:02:14.635 --> 00:02:17.444
Otherwise z becomes the left child

00:02:17.527 --> 00:02:19.758
of y if its key is smaller

00:02:19.758 --> 00:02:22.320
and the right child otherwise.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.177
InsertDemo builds the tree from

00:02:25.258 --> 00:02:27.523
before and inserts 13

00:02:27.523 --> 00:02:30.677
it lands as the left child of 15

00:02:30.677 --> 00:02:33.832
and the parent of 13 is 15.

00:02:33.832 --> 00:02:36.177
The loop goes down one path

00:02:36.177 --> 00:02:38.361
and the rest is O(1)

00:02:38.361 --> 00:02:41.030
so insertion takes O(h) time.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.510
The slides ask you to build

00:02:44.618 --> 00:02:49.236
the tree for 15, 6, 18, 3

00:02:49.236 --> 00:02:53.211
7, 17, 20, 2, 4.

00:02:53.211 --> 00:02:57.722
The first key, 15, becomes the root

00:02:57.722 --> 00:03:01.052
and it stays the root forever.

00:03:01.052 --> 00:03:04.274
6 is smaller: it goes left.

00:03:04.274 --> 00:03:06.530
18 goes right.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.590
3 goes left, then left.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:12.500
7 goes left, then right.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.515
17 and 20 go below 18.

00:03:16.515 --> 00:03:20.228
2 walks down to 3's left side

00:03:20.228 --> 00:03:23.440
and 4 to 3's right side.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.944
Each later key walks down from

00:03:26.016 --> 00:03:28.026
the root and becomes a leaf

00:03:28.026 --> 00:03:30.682
and InsertDemo prints the same tree.

00:03:30.682 --> 00:03:32.190
Its height is 3.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.563
What if the key is already in the tree?

00:03:35.563 --> 00:03:38.428
Our code sends equal keys to the right

00:03:38.499 --> 00:03:40.433
because z.key < x.key

00:03:40.505 --> 00:03:41.866
is false.

00:03:41.937 --> 00:03:44.014
So a second copy lands in the

00:03:44.086 --> 00:03:46.019
right subtree of the first.

00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:47.810
Then the property reads

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.672
smaller keys on the left

00:03:49.672 --> 00:03:52.250
greater or equal keys on the right.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.506
Most libraries do neither.

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:56.411
A TreeSet simply ignores

00:03:56.485 --> 00:03:58.024
a key it already has

00:03:58.097 --> 00:04:00.076
and a TreeMap replaces the

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.689
value stored with it.

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:02.568
To do that

00:04:02.568 --> 00:04:05.720
stop the walk when the two keys are equal.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.715
The same keys inserted in different

00:04:08.784 --> 00:04:10.717
orders give different trees.

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:13.477
Insert 1 to 5 in increasing order

00:04:13.546 --> 00:04:15.340
each key becomes the right

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:17.065
child of the one before

00:04:17.065 --> 00:04:19.205
a chain of height n - 1

00:04:19.205 --> 00:04:20.309
the worst case.

00:04:20.309 --> 00:04:22.793
Insert 3, 1, 4, 2, 5

00:04:22.793 --> 00:04:24.380
and the tree is bushy.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.840
HeightDemo does this with 1000 keys.

00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:30.062
In sorted order the height is

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.988
999

00:04:32.062 --> 00:04:34.135
so a search may walk through

00:04:34.209 --> 00:04:35.616
all 1000 nodes.

00:04:35.616 --> 00:04:38.134
Shuffled, the height is 18

00:04:38.134 --> 00:04:40.430
twice the best possible, 9

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.170
and 50 times less than the chain.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.263
Input that arrives already sorted

00:04:46.346 --> 00:04:48.174
is common in practice

00:04:48.174 --> 00:04:51.000
that is why balanced trees exist.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.200
Deletion is harder

00:04:53.285 --> 00:04:55.417
we must remove a node and

00:04:55.502 --> 00:04:57.378
keep the BST property.

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:59.851
How hard depends on how many

00:04:59.936 --> 00:05:01.982
children the node z has.

00:05:01.982 --> 00:05:04.881
Our example tree has 12 keys.

00:05:04.881 --> 00:05:08.547
Case 1: z has no children, it is a leaf

00:05:08.547 --> 00:05:09.826
like 13.

00:05:09.826 --> 00:05:11.276
Just cut it off

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:14.260
12's right child becomes null.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.052
Case 2: z has one child.

00:05:17.052 --> 00:05:20.455
16 has only a right child, 20.

00:05:20.455 --> 00:05:21.646
Splice z out

00:05:21.646 --> 00:05:25.389
link z's parent directly to z's only child

00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:27.856
and set that child's parent.

00:05:27.856 --> 00:05:31.089
15's right child becomes 20.

00:05:31.174 --> 00:05:33.300
The child's whole subtree

00:05:33.385 --> 00:05:35.002
moves up one level

00:05:35.087 --> 00:05:37.639
with 18 and 23

00:05:37.724 --> 00:05:39.000
still below it.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.706
Why is that safe?

00:05:40.786 --> 00:05:43.419
Every key in that subtree lies on

00:05:43.499 --> 00:05:45.972
the same side of every ancestor

00:05:46.051 --> 00:05:48.126
of 16 as 16 did

00:05:48.126 --> 00:05:51.077
all of them are larger than 15.

00:05:51.077 --> 00:05:53.710
So the BST property still holds.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:57.754
Case 3: z has two children, like 5.

00:05:57.754 --> 00:05:59.816
We cannot splice it out

00:05:59.816 --> 00:06:01.878
its parent has one link

00:06:01.878 --> 00:06:05.178
but 5 has two subtrees to hand over.

00:06:05.178 --> 00:06:05.920
Instead

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.137
find a key that can take 5's place.

00:06:09.219 --> 00:06:11.941
It must be larger than everything

00:06:12.024 --> 00:06:15.323
on the left, and smaller than everything

00:06:15.405 --> 00:06:16.890
else on the right.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.225
That is the successor y

00:06:19.225 --> 00:06:22.038
the minimum of z's right subtree

00:06:22.038 --> 00:06:25.181
go right to 12, then left to 10

00:06:25.181 --> 00:06:26.670
then left to 6.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:28.572
6 has no left child

00:06:28.572 --> 00:06:30.640
so the walk stops there.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.027
So: copy y's key into z

00:06:33.027 --> 00:06:35.404
and then delete the node y.

00:06:35.404 --> 00:06:38.545
y has no left child by construction

00:06:38.545 --> 00:06:42.196
because we went left until there was none.

00:06:42.196 --> 00:06:45.506
So removing y is case 1 or case 2

00:06:45.506 --> 00:06:47.544
never case 3 again

00:06:47.544 --> 00:06:50.430
case 3 recurses at most once.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.238
Before we run it, predict.

00:06:53.238 --> 00:06:54.609
In delete(5)

00:06:54.609 --> 00:06:57.607
which node object leaves the tree

00:06:57.607 --> 00:06:59.664
the one that held 5

00:06:59.664 --> 00:07:01.891
or the one that held 6?

00:07:01.891 --> 00:07:04.290
Pause the video and decide.

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:11.562
6 is copied into the node that held 5.

00:07:11.647 --> 00:07:13.794
The node object that held

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:17.571
5 stays where it was, and now holds 6.

00:07:17.571 --> 00:07:20.490
The old node 6 leaves the tree

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.980
it is spliced out, case 2

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:26.070
and 10's left child becomes 7.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.817
DeleteDemo prints the same tree.

00:07:28.817 --> 00:07:30.165
So the answer is

00:07:30.165 --> 00:07:32.787
the node that held 6 disappears.

00:07:32.787 --> 00:07:35.484
For a set of integers that is fine.

00:07:35.484 --> 00:07:38.180
But a reference to the node of 6

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:40.352
kept elsewhere in a program

00:07:40.352 --> 00:07:42.674
now points at a detached node.

00:07:42.749 --> 00:07:45.146
So CLRS moves the successor node

00:07:45.221 --> 00:07:47.468
itself into z's place instead

00:07:47.468 --> 00:07:49.940
with a helper called TRANSPLANT.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.293
In Java

00:07:51.293 --> 00:07:54.964
delete(z) first handles case 3.

00:07:54.964 --> 00:07:57.283
If z has both children

00:07:57.283 --> 00:08:01.630
y is BstMinMax.findMin(z.right), the successor

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:04.045
which has no left child.

00:08:04.045 --> 00:08:06.557
z.key = y.key

00:08:06.557 --> 00:08:10.131
and then z = y: from now on

00:08:10.131 --> 00:08:12.160
we remove y instead.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.624
What remains is always the same job

00:08:15.624 --> 00:08:18.910
remove a node with at most one child.

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:22.455
Cases 1 and 2 share their code.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:24.488
The only child is z.left

00:08:24.573 --> 00:08:28.300
if there is one, otherwise z.right.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:30.925
In case 1 it is simply null.

00:08:30.925 --> 00:08:32.704
If there is a child

00:08:32.704 --> 00:08:35.330
its parent becomes z's parent.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.570
If z has no parent, z is the root

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.630
and the field root changes instead.

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:45.370
Otherwise, if z is its parent's left child

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.855
z.parent.left becomes the child

00:08:48.855 --> 00:08:52.000
if not, z.parent.right does.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:54.629
The slides write case 3

00:08:54.714 --> 00:08:56.233
with two branches.

00:08:56.233 --> 00:08:58.428
Both are this one splice

00:08:58.428 --> 00:09:00.200
y has no left child

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.070
so its only child is y.right.

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:06.610
Deleting the root works the same way.

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:08.690
15 has two children.

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.770
Its successor is 16

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:13.490
the minimum of the right subtree.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:16.130
16 is copied into the root

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:19.010
and the old 16 is spliced out

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.650
its right child 20 moves up.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:22.905
One pitfall.

00:09:22.905 --> 00:09:24.414
When z is the root

00:09:24.414 --> 00:09:27.129
there is no z.parent to update

00:09:27.129 --> 00:09:30.373
the tree's root field must change instead

00:09:30.448 --> 00:09:32.258
and the child that moves

00:09:32.334 --> 00:09:33.993
up gets a null parent.

00:09:34.069 --> 00:09:36.860
Code that sets the root and then also

00:09:36.935 --> 00:09:39.877
asks whether z is z.parent.left

00:09:39.952 --> 00:09:42.366
throws a NullPointerException.

00:09:42.366 --> 00:09:45.760
The parent update belongs in an else branch.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:47.194
The cost.

00:09:47.288 --> 00:09:50.228
Cases 1 and 2 take constant

00:09:50.323 --> 00:09:52.314
time once z is found.

00:09:52.409 --> 00:09:54.969
Case 3 adds a walk down

00:09:55.064 --> 00:09:56.676
to the successor.

00:09:56.676 --> 00:10:00.184
And finding z from a key is O(h).

00:10:00.184 --> 00:10:02.460
So deletion is O(h).

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:04.572
There is a subtle problem.

00:10:04.639 --> 00:10:06.875
Every case 3 deletion takes a

00:10:06.943 --> 00:10:08.975
node out of z's right subtree.

00:10:08.975 --> 00:10:11.956
After many deletions mixed with insertions

00:10:11.956 --> 00:10:13.921
the right subtrees thin out

00:10:13.989 --> 00:10:16.292
and the tree leans to the left and

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:18.528
gets taller than it needs to be.

00:10:18.596 --> 00:10:21.306
Choosing at random between the successor

00:10:21.374 --> 00:10:23.135
and the predecessor helps

00:10:23.135 --> 00:10:24.490
but proves nothing.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.366
The rigorous solution is a balanced

00:10:27.438 --> 00:10:28.809
binary search tree

00:10:28.881 --> 00:10:31.407
which repairs its shape after every

00:10:31.479 --> 00:10:33.139
insertion and deletion

00:10:33.139 --> 00:10:35.304
AVL trees in Lecture 15

00:10:35.304 --> 00:10:38.190
and red-black trees in Lecture 16.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:39.634
Both add rotations

00:10:39.634 --> 00:10:42.520
and keep the height O(log n) always.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:43.880
To sum up.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:46.609
1: insertion searches with x and

00:10:46.687 --> 00:10:48.481
the trailing pointer y

00:10:48.481 --> 00:10:50.820
and hangs a new leaf below y

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:52.145
in O(h) time.

00:10:52.145 --> 00:10:54.251
2: deletion cuts a leaf

00:10:54.251 --> 00:10:56.980
splices out a node with one child

00:10:57.058 --> 00:10:59.163
and for two children copies

00:10:59.241 --> 00:11:00.801
the successor's key

00:11:00.801 --> 00:11:03.140
whose node has no left child.

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:04.044
3:

00:11:04.044 --> 00:11:07.921
the order of insertion decides the shape.

00:11:07.921 --> 00:11:10.506
Sorted input makes a chain

00:11:10.598 --> 00:11:13.182
and random input gives

00:11:13.275 --> 00:11:15.490
O(log n) height on average.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:16.665
Try this.

00:11:16.665 --> 00:11:19.871
Take the running tree of part two

00:11:19.871 --> 00:11:22.253
with 15 at the root.

00:11:22.253 --> 00:11:25.550
Delete 6, which has two children.

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.566
What is the new tree?

00:11:27.566 --> 00:11:30.680
Hint: find 6's successor first.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:32.016
In part 4:

00:11:32.016 --> 00:11:34.821
do insertions and deletions commute?

00:11:34.821 --> 00:11:37.854
Then sorting with a binary search tree

00:11:37.854 --> 00:11:41.190
and the lowest common ancestor of two keys.

